{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>And Terah died in Haran.</b> After Abram left Haran and came to the land of Canaan, he was there more than sixty years — for it is written ״וְאַבְרָם בֶּן חָמֵשׁ שָׁנִים וְשִׁבְעִים שָׁנָה בְּצֵאתוֹ מֵחָרָן״ and Terah was seventy years old when Abram was born — that makes 145 for Terah. When Abram left Haran, many of his years still remained. And why did Scripture precede the death of Terah to the departure of Abram? So that the matter would not be publicized to all and they would say: Abram did not uphold the honor of his father — he left him old and went on his way. Therefore Scripture called him dead, for the wicked, even in their lifetime, are called dead, and the righteous, even in their death, are called alive, as it is written ״וּבְנָיָהוּ בֶּן יְהוֹיָדָע בֶּן אִישׁ חַי״ (II Samuel 23)." "(<b>In Haran.</b> The nun is inverted, to tell you: until Abram — the wrath of the Omnipresent was kindled [חֲרוֹן אַף].)"], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T17:26:15.207930670Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}